WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.425
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:09.690
BENJAMIN TREYNOR
SLOSS: What is SRE?

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:13.860
Well, SRE has become a lot of
things over the past 15 years.

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:18.390
But when we started it in 2004,
it was a pretty simple concept.

00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:20.540
It's what you get when
you treat operations

00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:22.550
as a software problem
and you staff it

00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:24.350
with software engineers.

00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:26.000
And everything else
came from that.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:28.922
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:36.610
SRE is a way of thinking
and approaching production.

00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:39.040
It's a set of principles
and practices.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:40.950
And so in a sense,
most people who

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:43.650
are engineers who could
be developing a system

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:48.130
can also be an SRE working
on that same system.

00:00:48.130 --> 00:00:50.520
And when we talk to
somebody about joining SRE,

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:53.290
we're really looking
for a few things.

00:00:53.290 --> 00:00:56.280
First of all, do they
have a natural tendency

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:59.130
to avoid toil and want to
automate it away and make

00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:01.050
a computer do it?

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:04.170
If they're allergic to doing
things by hand over and over

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:05.820
again, and if they
have the computer

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:09.170
skills to take the work that
they've been doing by hand

00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:11.460
and make a computer
do it instead,

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:14.670
they got a good
character trait for SRE.

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:17.560
Secondly, can they
handle complexity?

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:20.130
And in particular, can
they take a complex

00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:22.590
and maybe not
well-defined problem

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:25.860
and come up with a scalable,
technically reasonable solution

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:28.110
for it and implement
that solution?

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:31.530
We call those skills
non-abstract large systems

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:32.430
design.

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:34.770
And it's a skill that
pretty much every SRE

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:37.990
uses every single day.

00:01:37.990 --> 00:01:40.440
Finally, we look for
an innate curiosity

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:42.330
about how things work.

00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:44.340
We're looking for people
who will learn over

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:47.880
the course of their
lifetime in SRE

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:52.630
and understand systems as they
develop and as they change.

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:56.003
When we find someone
with those attributes,

00:01:56.003 --> 00:01:57.420
we have someone
with the potential

00:01:57.420 --> 00:01:59.413
to be a really excellent SRE.

00:01:59.413 --> 00:02:01.080
JENNIFER PETOFF: So
once we find someone

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:03.150
who generally fits
that shape, that's

00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:04.860
when the real work begins.

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:06.690
When it comes to
SRE, there's a lot

00:02:06.690 --> 00:02:10.289
to learn, from reliability best
practices and design patterns,

00:02:10.289 --> 00:02:13.050
to the ins and outs of
complex production systems,

00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:16.590
to incident management
best practices and more.

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:19.590
Over the years, we've developed
hundreds of hours and thousands

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:22.980
of pages of material to help
develop those inclinations

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:25.830
into excellent
production skills.

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:28.800
Once someone gets up to speed
on an SRE team at Google,

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:31.870
then we spend a lot of effort
on continuing education,

00:02:31.870 --> 00:02:34.530
things like rotations
and short-term projects.

00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:37.290
Great SREs aren't hired,
they're actually trained.

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:39.270
And their skills
are honed over time.

00:02:39.270 --> 00:02:42.048
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:46.530
BENJAMIN LUTCH: Google
was a lot smaller than it

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:48.150
is today when we started SRE.

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:51.270
In the beginning, SRE was just a
few people in a couple offices.

00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:55.290
Today, we're more than 2,500
folks spread around the world.

00:02:55.290 --> 00:02:58.678
Basic SRE principles scale
from very small to super large.

00:02:58.678 --> 00:03:00.345
As long as you keep
them in mind and let

00:03:00.345 --> 00:03:02.040
them guide you along
the way, you'll

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:03.670
end up in the right place.

00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:05.920
If I were starting another
company from scratch again,

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:07.795
I would definitely be
doing SRE from day one.

00:03:07.795 --> 00:03:10.718
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:03:13.443 --> 00:03:15.360
BETSY BEYER: For a long
time, we wouldn't talk

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:17.430
about SRE outside of Google.

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:19.110
It was the secret sauce.

00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:22.320
But a few years ago, we
started to rethink that.

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:24.300
Now we spend a lot
of time working

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:27.360
to build a large and
vibrant SRE community.

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:30.000
We've published
several books and also

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.790
dozens of articles,
videos, and trainings,

00:03:32.790 --> 00:03:34.650
with much more to come.

00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:37.560
Almost all of this material
is available for free

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:40.600
on Google SRE's home page.

00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:43.170
We know that an important
part of SRE's value

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:45.557
is creating something
meaningful and lasting

00:03:45.557 --> 00:03:46.515
for a larger community.

00:03:46.515 --> 00:03:49.265
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:53.600
BENJAMIN TREYNOR
SLOSS: Well, that's

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:55.680
the uncomfortably exciting part.

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:57.770
I have a lot of
ideas, but the truth

00:03:57.770 --> 00:04:00.290
is I don't really
know for certain.

00:04:00.290 --> 00:04:03.230
Now that SRE has grown to be
a global community and not

00:04:03.230 --> 00:04:06.050
just a Google thing, the
future of the profession

00:04:06.050 --> 00:04:08.840
is determined by the progress
and innovations of everyone

00:04:08.840 --> 00:04:10.070
in SRE.

00:04:10.070 --> 00:04:12.410
And if you had asked
me in 2004 what

00:04:12.410 --> 00:04:16.010
would become of the SRE team, I
would not have predicted this.

00:04:16.010 --> 00:04:18.709
I thought it was going
to be a very small niche

00:04:18.709 --> 00:04:21.149
part of computer science.

00:04:21.149 --> 00:04:23.510
So what I do predict
is that the community

00:04:23.510 --> 00:04:27.470
of smart and motivated people
who now make up the global SRE

00:04:27.470 --> 00:04:30.140
teams will continue to
come up with new ideas.

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:31.370
They'll continue to innovate.

00:04:31.370 --> 00:04:34.190
They'll continue to advance
the state of the art

00:04:34.190 --> 00:04:36.750
and advance what is possible.

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:39.890
And I'll be part of that,
but just part of it.

00:04:39.890 --> 00:04:43.760
So really, I'm looking forward
to seeing what you all create.

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:46.810
[MUSIC PLAYING]

